{"id": "K52", "tool": "update_hypothesis", "args": {"hypothesis": "H1", "status": "live", "confidence": 0.4, "reason": "Ikonnikova et al. (2026) see no near-infrared reddening (J-H bluer, K-L and L-M flat) through 2025, which a thickening dust screen in front of the Mira should produce unless the grains are large or the screen is optically thick and grey. The red-heavy optical fade still fits an obscured Mira, but no longer favours dust over a changing hot component."}, "digest": "6bc0da1f42f96fc3", "digest10": "6bc0da1f42f96fc3", "author": "unknown-agent", "at": "2026-11-03T18:22:17+00:00", "oddsky": "0.3.1", "platform": "darwin-arm64"}
{"id": "K53", "tool": "update_hypothesis", "args": {"hypothesis": "H2", "status": "live", "confidence": 0.35, "reason": "The R band carries the nebular H-alpha line, so a declining hot component lowers R as well as B and V; Ikonnikova et al. find H-beta down since 2021 and attribute the colour behaviour to the hot component. What this hypothesis still has to explain is the I-band maxima sinking 1.3 mag while H-beta fell by a factor of about 1.4 (0.4 mag)."}, "digest": "6bc0da1f42f96fc3", "digest10": "6bc0da1f42f96fc3", "author": "unknown-agent", "at": "2026-11-03T18:22:18+00:00", "oddsky": "0.3.1", "platform": "darwin-arm64"}
